{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for lane A at exact head 315a857685f3b639236e70236887c1ecb5817b56. Primary basis: packages/room/README.md; all 52 submitted artifacts are bound to this review. The requested full scope remains in place.\n\nIndependent validation: clean npm ci --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund; root typecheck passed; the candidate's 66 Node and 129 workerd tests passed. Eight additional diagnostics passed under real Durable Object SQLite/workerd, reproducing the defects below. They are in the isolated checkout /tmp/artroom-checker-room-A/packages/room/test/workerd/checker-review.test.ts; run from packages/room with npx vitest run --config vitest.workers.config.ts test/workerd/checker-review.test.ts. These tests assert the defective outcomes, so passing them proves reproduction, not correction. No production deployment, credentials, or live Artifacts mutation was used.\n\nP1.1 — A refused room-custody redemption consumes the invitation.\npackages/room/src/requests.ts:234–258 commits join, then separately submits the session delegation. For an invited agent, a valid roster refusal rule blocking op=delegate permits join and then refuses delegation. redeem returns that refusal, but the log gained the accepted join and a recorded delegation refusal, and the invitation is used. Retrying returns invitation-invalid; the caller never obtained a bearer or the held member key. This contradicts R-CRED-9's no-entry/no-consumption refused-redemption outcome and also exposes interruption between the two admissions. Evaluate the complete redemption before consuming the invitation, and seal its required entries and held-key/session state atomically with no await in the write transaction, or establish an explicitly permitted durable completion protocol that cannot strand a consumed invitation. Cover refusal, policy runtime failure, interruption between stages, and response-loss recovery without exposing held keys.\n\nP1.2 — A previously compromised key can become the recovery key and sign admin operations.\npackages/room/src/admission.ts:311–314 checks only member key bindings when rotating recovery; packages/room/src/authority.ts:73–76 accepts current recovery without consulting revocation. Revoke a fresh unbound key as compromised, rotate recovery to it, then sign set-role with it: both operations are accepted, the latter via recovery. R-ADM-3 requires the signing key not to have been revoked for any reason. Refuse rotation to every previously revoked key, and enforce revocation for recovery authority as a backstop. Test compromised and retired keys, bound and unbound, alongside a valid fresh rotation.\n\nP1.3 — A delegation that expires during policy evaluation still authorizes a new act.\npackages/room/src/admission.ts:190–201 decides with a captured clock, awaits policy, then guards only the log head before commit. In the diagnostic, delegated claim enters policy before expiry, the gate waits while the Room clock advances past expiry, and no intervening entry is written. The claim seals accepted via delegation after expiry. Rejudge time-sensitive authority synchronously with the current clock immediately before sealing; do not consume an idempotency key or append an entry for expired delegation authority. Audit invitation and other relevant deadlines through the same final admission boundary. Keep the existing gapless/concurrent-log tests, and add elapsed-time-without-log-change cases.\n\nP1.4 — Policy activation preserves evidence qualifications for a different obligation.\npackages/room/src/core.ts:307–340 replaces requirement specs but keeps evidence.qualifies and carried assignments; packages/room/src/obligations.ts:129–150 trusts those IDs. Bob approves a same-id requirement from @bob; activate a policy changing that same ID to @carol and finish recomputation. The proposal reports from=[@carol], state=met, using Bob's evidence. Requirement IDs are not proof that the requirement's reviewer, paths, count, self-approval, checker configuration or carry conditions are unchanged. Recompute applicability/qualification and carry under the new requirement and policy, using the evidence's recorded admission authority rather than retroactively substituting today's role. Keep compromise and sole-admin exceptions. Test changes to principals/owners, paths, counts, self approval, checker inputs/configuration, and carried reviews, then verify land cannot bypass the new requirement. Record the recomputation decisions and changed obligations using the amended event contract when adopted.\n\nP1.5 — Lost successful log-push responses permanently wedge publication.\npackages/room/src/room.ts:209–245 has no durable pending publication. log_commit and published_through move only after push and read-back. The diagnostic lets push apply remotely and then lose its reply; local state stays at the old parent. Advance the clock, append a legitimate entry, and retry: a newly built commit is pushed against the old lease and fails forever. Persist the exact candidate commit, cohort and expected parent before remote mutation; read back after ambiguous failures and resume that same pending publication across restart before choosing another cohort. Advance publishedThrough only after confirming that exact commit. Integrate the durable lane L publisher rather than retaining a second independent publisher state machine. Test lost push and read-back replies, restart, entries appended during recovery, and an unexpected remote writer.\n\nP2.6 — Durable pending workspaces are never resumed.\npackages/room/src/requests.ts:75–81 returns an existing pending operation without restarting it, and core.nextAlarm/Room.work never scan pending workspaces. The diagnostic inserts the legitimate persisted state at the post-insert/pre-kick interruption boundary for op_ws_2_1. Running the alarm and repeating the authorized request returns pending; ensureFork is never called. Make workspace creation durable alarm work, resume the same operation and lease after restart, and fence stale-holder/token access. Test interruption before starting ensureFork and after remote fork creation but before updating the operation.\n\nP2.7 — A repeated approval seals an incorrect obligations-met effect.\npackages/room/src/admission.ts:895–905 uses evidenceActs.length+1 instead of the resulting distinct-member status. With count=2, Bob approves twice. The second sealed receipt says met=[obl_two], while the proposal correctly remains open. Existing R-OBL-4 tests check only the projection and miss the false immutable receipt. Build effects from the same actual post-admission status computation used by the projection, including latest verdicts, distinct members and carried evidence. Record met/open transitions accurately for repeated approvals and objections. Test the sealed entry as well as the projection and landing behavior.\n\nP2.8 — Attention cursors silently skip work at a page boundary.\npackages/room/src/reads.ts:190–210 orders by (seq,id), but its cursor records only seq and the next page filters seq \u003e last. Two review obligations addressed to Bob create two items at the proposal's same sequence. With limit=1, page one says more=true; page two is empty and the other requirement is lost. Encode a stable (seq,id) attention position, and preserve legacy cursor behavior if already exposed. Audit updateAfter's 100-item attention cap for the same truncation problem; an update cursor must not advance beyond unseen attention. Test multiple obligations and notifications from one entry, overlapping principals, and small page limits.\n\nP1.9 — Lane A is a foundation, and its contracted integration/acceptance scope is not complete.\nREADME accurately names the unfinished work, but request 7430cf7c explicitly requires landing to call lane B's engine, even on its branch before merge, plus every Room acceptance case. packages/room/src/config.ts instead selects UnwiredLanding/UnwiredArtifacts in production, while the workerd suite exercises a separate MemoryLanding replica. That cannot validate restart-safe Room/B reservation, publication and compromise handling. Add the real narrow adapters and shared SQLite integration tests; complete cross-integration check carry, filtered checker inputs and Room authority replay as required by R-CARRY/R-LOG and the section 23 matrix. Apply the adopted contract amendments to founding, bearer RPC and byte subscriptions, recomputation/readiness evidence and retry reasons. The founding repository authorization/exclusive-binding issues are already tracked in review 152f29e8 and its pending amendment revision. Keep MCP deployment work in its owning lanes, but validate the Room transport boundary with them. Neither missing integration nor a reduced test matrix becomes out of scope because of a schedule estimate.\n\nArchitecture: synchronous sealing and the trusted admission path are sound foundations; the policy adapter uses the real C runtime and retained replay contexts. Approval is blocked by admission-time authority drift, invalid evidence recomputation, incomplete B/L integration, and non-durable external work. One Room must remain the single repository sequencer, with durable completion through every interrupted external operation.\n\nSecurity: custody routing, strict JSON/signatures, secret scanning and holder-only token surfaces have useful coverage. The compromised recovery-key acceptance and delegation-expiry race violate authority invariants; partial redemption strands credentials. Production authorization and repository-exclusive founding must be established before canonical credentials are wired. These local findings do not claim a deployed exploit.\n\nSimplification: use the real B landing engine and L publisher through thin adapters; keep memory doubles focused on transport faults rather than duplicating state machines. Centralize obligation status/transition calculation and durable alarm work instead of separate qualification, effect and projection logic. Preserve the complete scope and make the README's pending acceptance matrix explicit until that scope is demonstrated.\n\nPlease submit a new exact-head candidate addressing each finding with tests, updated artifact bases and an acceptance-case matrix distinguishing unit, real SQLite, real B/L integration and deployed evidence.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:bfc0f3a30be8d07c470f287a2d56e7f3d19d9e1a","binding":"assigned","head":"315a857685f3b639236e70236887c1ecb5817b56","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b4b68ceba96f6ab00f0096d0244a6f9744ea354a\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d2409eaede0361d81de52cb482c667967a64779c\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:cd54874484eedbd5e1af3589967c15f2cf0ce32d","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}